module Core

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   A rendering placed in a column - the other half of `printf`'s width, and the half that is not
   about numbers at all.
   ==========================================================================================

   `%08x` conflates two operations: writing a number with at least eight digits, and placing a
   rendering in a column eight units wide. `Format/Number.yana`'s `digits` is the first and this is
   the second, and keeping them apart is what lets each one be simple - `printf`'s zero flag has to
   be defined against its sign, against its precision and against left-justification, because it is
   trying to be both at once.

   **This works on anything that can be shown**, which is the whole reason it is a formatter of its
   own: `padded(name, 12)`, `padded(hex(x, digits: 8), 12)` and `padded(fixed(cost), 10)` are the
   same operation over three different renderings, and none of the three had to know about it.

   **A column is counted in code units.** That is what a monospaced terminal aligns and what every
   other function in this library counts, so it is the number a caller can predict. Text whose units
   are not its glyphs - a combining sequence, an East Asian width, a `\t` - is text this cannot align
   and neither can `printf`; what it promises is a count, not a typesetter.
-}

{-
   Where the fill goes, which is the same question as which edge the value sits against.

   `Leading` is the default because it is `printf`'s: `%12s` puts the fill in front, and a column of
   numbers is the case that has to line up. `Trailing` is what a column of names wants, and
   `Balanced` is a heading over either - split evenly, with the odd unit going after the value, so a
   caller who centers two strings of different parity does not get them off by a unit in opposite
   directions.
-}
pub data Padding
  = @value(0) Leading
  | @value(1) Trailing
  | @value(2) Balanced

{-
   A rendering and the column it goes in.

   The subject is `'a` like every other formatter here, so `padded(hex(n), 10)` refers to the
   `NumberFormat` rather than copying it and the `NumberFormat` refers to `n`. Two borrows deep, and
   the return-root check bounds both: neither may leave the expression that built it.

   `fill` is a `CodeUnit` rather than a `Maybe` for the reason `NumberFormat`'s `group` is one, and
   here the default is a real space rather than a sentinel - a column padded with nothing is a column
   that does not exist, so there is no "no fill" case to spell.

   `width` is a *minimum*. A rendering wider than the column is written whole and pushes the row out,
   which is the only answer that does not lose data; a caller who wants it clipped is asking for a
   different operation and should say so where the text is made.
-}
{-
   **A column is not a style, and this is the one formatter in the library that is not.**

   Every other one describes how to render a *leaf* - a number, a date, a run of bytes - so its
   settings are a recipe both directions can read. A column describes what to do with a rendering
   somebody else made, which is why it works on anything showable and why `padded(hex(x, digits: 8),
   12)` is two of them nested. That nesting is what keeps it out of `Formatted`: the generic
   `Show(Formatted(s, a))` calls `showWith`, an instance keyed on a column would call `show` back at
   `a`, and with `a` unknown the compiler cannot see the pair bottom out - "polymorphically
   recursive - it would need endlessly many specializations" is exactly what it says. A record of its
   own breaks the cycle, because `Aligned(Aligned(a))` is a depth the type states.

   It has no reader either, and never did: a column is padding a caller added and a reader would have
   to guess how much of it to take back. So there is nothing here for a shared recipe to share.
-}
pub data Aligned(a) {
    value: 'a,
    width: Size,
    padding: Padding = Leading,
    fill: CodeUnit = 32
}

-- The constructor, which is what a call site writes. `padded(x, 12)` right-aligns in twelve units;
-- the two other placements and a different fill unit are named arguments, since a call site reading
-- `padded(name, 12, Trailing)` says less than `padded(name, 12, padding: Trailing)` does.
pub fn (Show(a)) padded(value: 'a, width: Size, padding: Padding = Leading, fill: CodeUnit = 32) -> Aligned(a) =
    Aligned {value: value, width: width, padding: padding, fill: fill}

-- `count` fill units, and nothing at all for a count that is not positive - which is the ordinary
-- case of a rendering at or over its column's width.
fn pushFill(&to: String, fill: CodeUnit, count: Size):
    let &left = count

    while left > 0:
        String.pushUnit(to, fill :: Int)
        left = left - 1

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Writing the column
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   **`Trailing` writes straight into the destination and the other two cannot**, which is the one
   thing about this instance worth reading. Fill that goes *after* the value is fill appended once
   the value's own units are counted, so the value is written where it belongs and the position it
   started at answers how wide it was. Fill that goes *before* it needs that width first, and the
   only ways to have it are to render twice or to render somewhere else - so those two render into a
   scratch `String` sized by the value's own `showBound` and append it after the fill.

   The scratch is one allocation on a native target and a host string on JS, and it is spent only by
   the placements that need it. A `Trailing` column costs exactly what the value costs plus its fill.
-}
instance (Show(a)) Show(Aligned(a)):
    fn show(value: Aligned(a), &to: String):
        if value.padding is Trailing:
            let start = length(to)
            show(value.value, to)
            pushFill(to, value.fill, value.width - (length(to) - start))
            return

        let &rendered = String.ofCapacity(formatBound(showBound(value.value)))
        show(value.value, rendered)

        let short = value.width - length(rendered)
        let before = if value.padding is Balanced then short / 2 else short

        pushFill(to, value.fill, before)
        String.push(to, rendered)
        pushFill(to, value.fill, short - before)

    {-
       The column's width or the value's own bound, whichever is larger - and `Nothing` when the
       value has none.

       A bound may be generous and may not be short, so a value that cannot say how wide it gets
       makes the whole column unbounded: answering `width` there would promise a size the very next
       long string breaks. `formatBound` turns that into a seed of zero and the appends grow past it,
       which is Implementation-Storage.md part 8's strategy (c) reached the way every other
       unbounded hole reaches it.
    -}
    fn showBound(value: Aligned(a)) -> Maybe(Size) = match showBound(value.value):
        Just(units) -> Just(if units > value.width then units else value.width)
        Nothing -> Nothing

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   `Clipped` - a rendering cut to a column, with a marker where it was cut
   ==========================================================================================

   The other half of `Aligned`, and the half it deliberately does not do: a column is a *minimum*
   width there, so a rendering wider than its column pushes the row out, and the note above says a
   caller who wants it clipped is asking for a different operation. This is that operation, and the
   pair is what a table cell actually wants - `padded(clipped(name, 20), 20)` is a column exactly
   twenty units wide whatever the name is, which neither of them can promise alone.

   **The marker is inside the width and not beside it**, which is the whole of what "clipped to
   twenty" has to mean: the result is at most twenty units, so a text that is cut gets seventeen of
   its own and three of `...`. A marker written outside the width would make the clipped row wider
   than the one that fit, which is the one thing a table cannot have.

   Like `Aligned`, this is a record of its own rather than a style over `Formatted`: it works on
   anything showable, it nests (`clipped(padded(x, 8), 6)` is legal and means what it says), and it
   has no reader, because a clipped text has lost what it clipped.
-}
pub data Clipped(a) {
    value: 'a,
    width: Size,
    ellipsis: Bool = True
}

{-
   The marker is a flag and not a string, and the reason is the loan rather than a preference.

   A `marker: 'String` field would make this two borrows deep, which `Joined` in `Format/List.yana`
   is and is fine - but the marker wants a *default*, and a borrow cannot have one: a `return`
   parameter is one the result may be rooted in, so a call site that left it out would have no
   storage of its own for the result to stay live in, and the compiler says so where it is written.
   Writing the literal inside the constructor instead is the same refusal read the other way - a
   borrow rooted in storage the caller does not own.

   So the choice is a marker on every call site or a marker this file writes, and the second is what
   `clipped(name, 20)` should say. `...` and not the single ellipsis unit, because `…` is one code
   unit on JS and three UTF-8 bytes natively, so a column counted in code units would come out a
   different width on the two targets - and this whole file counts code units. A caller who wants
   another marker clips to `width - n` with `ellipsis: False` and writes their own after it, which is
   two formatters and no new type.
-}
pub fn (Show(a)) clipped(value: 'a, width: Size, ellipsis: Bool = True) -> Clipped(a) =
    Clipped {value: value, width: width, ellipsis: ellipsis}

-- The marker's own width, which is the one number the two halves of the instance below share.
fn clipMarker(ellipsis: Bool) -> Size = if ellipsis then 3 else 0

{-
   The rendering is made first and measured after, which is the same trade `Aligned`'s two
   fill-in-front placements make: there is no way to know how wide a value writes without writing it,
   and the sink cannot be un-appended. So it goes into a scratch `String` sized by the value's own
   `showBound` and what reaches the destination is a prefix of it.

   The scratch is one allocation natively and a host string on JS, and it is the price of the
   operation rather than of this implementation.
-}
instance (Show(a)) Show(Clipped(a)):
    fn show(value: Clipped(a), &to: String):
        let &rendered = String.ofCapacity(formatBound(showBound(value.value)))
        show(value.value, rendered)

        if length(rendered) <= value.width:
            String.push(to, rendered)
            return

        -- A column narrower than the marker gets a prefix of the marker, which is the only answer
        -- that stays inside the width. It is not a case anybody writes on purpose; it is what stops
        -- `clipped(x, 1)` from being a subtraction below zero.
        let marker = clipMarker(value.ellipsis)
        if value.width <= marker:
            String.push(to, String.slice("...", 0, value.width))
            return

        String.push(to, String.slice(rendered, 0, value.width - marker))
        if value.ellipsis then String.push(to, "...")

    -- The width, exactly - and this is the one formatter in the library whose bound is tight rather
    -- than generous, because clipping is what makes it so: a rendering shorter than the column is
    -- written whole and is therefore under it, and a longer one is cut to it.
    --
    -- Clamped at zero, because a bound is a *size* and a negative width writes nothing rather than
    -- promising less than nothing. `padded` takes a negative width as "no column at all"; here it is
    -- "every unit clipped", and the two agree that the number itself is not a value to pass on.
    fn showBound(value: Clipped(a)) -> Maybe(Size) = Just(if value.width > 0 then value.width else 0)
